{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler of the County of Tripoli during the 12th century. He reigned from 1184 to 1202 a period marked by both significant territorial expansion and internal conflict. His reign is often considered a high point in the history of the County.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was the son of Raymond I Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county following his father's death in 1184. Immediately he faced challenges consolidating power and defending Tripoli against both internal factions and external threats particularly from the Saladin dynasty in Egypt. His early rule involved shrewd political maneuvering and military campaigns to secure Tripoli's borders and strengthen its position within the Levant. He skillfully navigated complex alliances often balancing relationships with both Muslim and Christian powers.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw a substantial increase in the size and influence of the County of Tripoli. He expanded its territory significantly acquiring new lands and strengthening its control over existing regions. He fostered economic growth through trade and agriculture making Tripoli a prosperous center in the region. He also invested in infrastructure building fortifications and improving the county's defenses. The county became a crucial bulwark against Muslim expansion in the Levant.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts* Raymond III was a skilled military leader. He engaged in numerous campaigns to defend Tripoli's borders and expand its territory. He fought against the Ayyubid Sultanate of Egypt several times. These conflicts were often protracted and costly but Raymond successfully defended Tripoli from numerous sieges. He also engaged in conflicts with other Crusader states such as the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Republic of Pisa. His military successes solidified Tripoli's position as a major power 